+# ttlcache: summary
+
+## What was wrong
+
+- `tests/test_cache.py` errored because `TTLCache` had no `clock=` parameter.
+- The reported bugs came from `get` in the old `ttlcache/cache.py`:
+  - **Loader runs twice:** there was no single-flight. Every concurrent miss called the loader.
+  - **Expired value served:** the check was `<= ttl`, so an entry exactly `ttl` old was served (the contract says it is expired). The value and its timestamp were also read outside the lock, so a concurrent `invalidate` or store could pair them up wrongly.
+  - Stats counters were updated outside the lock, so they were not exact under concurrency.
+  - `None` was treated as "no entry", so `None` values were never cached.
+
+## What changed
+
+- `ttlcache/cache.py`: rewritten to meet `docs/CONTRACT.md`.
+  - Single-flight loads: one `_Load` object per in-flight key. Waiters block on a condition variable and get the owner's result, or the very same exception object.
+  - The loader always runs outside the lock. Only short bookkeeping happens under it.
+  - Validity is decided under the lock with the injected `clock`: valid iff `age < ttl`.
+  - `max_size`: LRU via `OrderedDict`. Eviction skips keys with a load in flight.
+  - `stale_grace`: on failure, the owner and its waiters get the stale value if the entry is inside the grace period (decided once, when the failure is recorded).
+  - `timeout` on `get`: real-time wait on the condition. `timeout=0` raises at once. A timed-out waiter leaves the load alone.
+  - Deadlock detection: a wait-for graph (`_waiting`: thread -> load it blocks on; each load has an owner thread). Before a thread waits, it follows owner -> blocked-on -> owner. Reaching itself means a cycle, so it raises `DeadlockError`. Same-thread re-entry raises plain `RuntimeError`.
+  - `invalidate_all()` returns the count, `len()` counts stored entries, and `stats(extended=True)` adds the four extended counters.
+  - Constructor and `get` argument validation as in the contract.
+- `ttlcache/errors.py` (new): `DeadlockError(RuntimeError)`.
+- `ttlcache/stats.py`: the four extra counters and `snapshot(extended)`.
+- `ttlcache/__init__.py`: exports `DeadlockError`.
+- `tests/test_contract.py` (new): 28 tests for the guarantees (single load, no expired values, failure sharing, recursion, independence, invalidate, LRU, stale, timeout, deadlock, validation, stats keys). The existing `tests/test_cache.py` is untouched.
+
+## What I verified, and how
+
+- `python -m unittest` (Python 3.14.3, Linux): **37 tests, OK**. That is the 9 original tests plus the 28 new ones.
+- I ran `python -m unittest` 40 times in a loop with `subprocess`: all 40 passed.
+- Before the rewrite, the original 9 tests errored (`TypeError: unexpected keyword argument 'clock'`).
+- Two of my own tests had bugs (a wrong miss count and a wrong `invalidate_all` expectation, plus one deadlock test that could never form a cycle). I fixed the tests, not the expectations of the library behaviour.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- **CI:** I have no access to CI. I only ran the suite locally on one Python version (3.14.3). The code uses `threading.TIMEOUT_MAX`, which exists on 3.x, but other versions are untested.
+- **Concurrency coverage:** the tests are deterministic (events, not sleeps), but they only exercise a few interleavings. The "loader runs twice" report was rare, so I can't prove it is gone. I reasoned through the single-flight logic and the locking order; I did not run a stress test.
+- **Design choice:** all caches share one module-level lock and condition, so the deadlock graph spans caches. This serialises bookkeeping across caches (not loaders). Cross-cache deadlocks are not covered by a test.
+- **Test helpers:** two deadlock tests poll the internal `ttlcache.cache._waiting` dict (bounded) to know when a thread is blocked. If that internal name changes, those tests need updating.
+- **Choices the contract did not specify:**
+  - `bool` is rejected for `max_size` and `stale_grace`. `ttl=True` is still accepted, since `True > 0`.
+  - A `timeout` that is NaN raises `ValueError`.
+  - A loader raising `BaseException` (e.g. `KeyboardInterrupt`) is recorded as a failure and passed to waiters.
+- **Not changed:** `README.md` is outside the allowed files. It already documents the new options and is consistent with the code, so I left it alone.
